<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Holy Roman Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/962">962</a>–<a href="/years/1806">1806</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Holy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> was a complex political union of territories in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> that existed from 962 to 1806. It claimed succession to the Roman and Carolingian imperial traditions, with an elected emperor presiding over many semi-independent princes and cities. It has its own profile on the site and is also discussed as part of the legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire.jpg" alt="Holy Roman Empire" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Liuzzo, eagle by N3MO · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banner_of_the_Holy_Roman_Emperor_with_haloes_(1430-1806).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Charlemagne's coronation in 800 CE established the precedent of a Western imperial title that the <b>Holy Roman Empire</b> would claim to continue from 962 until 1806, and the ideal of a single Christian empire governing Latin Christendom remained a strong theme of European political thought throughout the Middle…</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>French, in its Picard and Walloon forms, was the dominant language, although the county lay within the <b>Holy Roman Empire</b>, and its elites moved easily between the French and imperial worlds through marriage and service.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The county that emerged was a small lordship of the <b>Holy Roman Empire</b>, centred on the castle and on Hechingen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the early 10th century, amid Magyar raids, the Luitpolding Arnulf made it a stem duchy within East Francia, and it remained one of the great duchies of the <b>Holy Roman Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>This marked the birth of the Duchy of Brabant as a territorial principality of the <b>Holy Roman Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The dukes were princes of the <b>Holy Roman Empire</b>, but the duchy’s ties to the Empire were loose and contested.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
